Subject: the allowlisted symbol block (ksym version 2) and the core tier

The kernel's block held every global, 146 KB on a 68060 kernel, filled
by mkksym into a 160 KiB reserve after the final link with run
addresses.  The Amix loader binds the kernel at a box-dependent address,
so an address written at link time would be wrong; and modules should
see only the published interface.  Version 2 (amix-dlm
docs/contracts/C3-2-symbols-and-ddi.md):

- mkksym -a writes the block of the allowlisted names as assembler,
  before the final link: each st_value, and kh_lo/kh_hi (stext, end), is
  a .long of the symbol, so the loader relocates it with .data; the hash
  depends on the names only.  mkksym -f fills st_size afterwards and
  fails if a listed name is undefined or of the other kind.
- st_other is the tier: 0 the DDI tier, 1 the core tier; the loader lets
  only a module whose .moddata says tier=core resolve a core-tier name.
- dlm_init checks the block against its own size, dlm_ksym_space; the
  stock dlmksym.s is an empty block, so a kernel linked without the
  generated one refuses modules.

30-module-core.md section 2, patch 5.  The state harness gains the tier.

